      id: 'q-rest-versioning',
      level: 'advanced',
      question: 'Design the REST conventions for a public Express API: status codes, versioning, and how you would make POST /payments safe to retry.',
      answer: 'Nouns for resources, plural, nested only one level. Correct codes: 201+Location on create, 204 on delete, 400/422 split for malformed vs invalid, 401/403 split, 409 for conflicts, 429 with Retry-After. Version in the path (/api/v1) via mounted routers — visible, cacheable, easy to run v1 and v2 side by side; never break a shipped version. Retryable POST: client sends an Idempotency-Key header; server atomically stores key → (in-progress/response) in Redis/DB with TTL; duplicate key returns the stored response instead of re-executing — exactly what Stripe does.',
      deepDive: 'The idempotency record must be written atomically with processing (or use a unique constraint) — check-then-act has a race where two retries both see "no key" and double-charge. That race is the expert follow-up.',
