      id: 'q-graceful-shutdown',
      level: 'expert',
      question: 'A deploy kills your Express pods mid-request and users see dropped connections. Design graceful shutdown.',
      answer: 'On SIGTERM: (1) flip the readiness probe to failing so the load balancer stops routing new traffic (keep liveness passing); (2) server.close() — stop accepting connections, let in-flight requests finish; close idle keep-alive sockets (closeIdleConnections); (3) drain background work, close DB pool and Redis clients; (4) exit 0 — all under a hard timeout (unref\'d timer → exit 1) inside the platform grace period (K8s default 30s, often with a preStop sleep so LB deregistration propagates before SIGTERM).',
      deepDive: 'Subtleties: keep-alive sockets with no active request hold server.close() open until their timeout — the closeIdleConnections step is what makes this work in practice. Requests started after SIGTERM but before LB deregistration still arrive; serve them (don\'t 503) during the drain window. Connection: close headers during drain accelerate it.',
      followUps: ['Readiness vs liveness probes — why must they diverge here?', 'How do long-lived SSE/WebSocket connections complicate this?'],
