  mentalModels: [
    {
      id: 'mm-assembly-line',
      title: 'Middleware as an Assembly Line',
      flow: ['Request enters the belt', 'Station 1: stamp ID, log', 'Station 2: security check (auth)', 'Station 3: unpack the box (body parse)', 'Final station: build response', 'Defect anywhere → eject to repair lane (error middleware)'],
      explanation: [
        'A request rides a conveyor belt through stations in fixed physical order. Each station inspects/modifies the item (req/res), then presses the green button (next()) — or pulls the red cord (next(err)) sending it straight to the repair lane, skipping every remaining normal station.',
        'A station that presses neither button stops the entire belt for that item — the hanging-request bug, visualized.',
        'Station order is the factory layout: you cannot unpack-then-inspect if inspection needs the contents — body parsing before validation, auth before protected handlers, repair lane physically last.',
      ],
      whatBreaksWithoutIt: 'Without the line model, middleware ordering bugs (body undefined, auth skipped, error handler never reached) look like mysteries instead of layout errors you can read straight off the app.use sequence.',
      interviewTip: 'Answer any "why doesn\'t my middleware work" question by reciting the registration order out loud — interviewers are usually testing exactly that.',
    },
    {
      id: 'mm-two-lanes',
      title: 'Two Lanes: Normal and Error',
      flow: ['next() → stay in normal lane', 'next(err) → switch to error lane', 'Normal lane: 3-arg layers only', 'Error lane: 4-arg layers only', 'Lanes never mix; error lane exits at the final handler'],
      explanation: [
        'The middleware stack is one road with two lanes. Every layer belongs to exactly one lane, determined by its arity — 3 parameters normal, 4 error.',
        'next() keeps you in the normal lane; next(err) is a permanent lane change for this request — the dispatcher will now only stop at 4-arg layers, in registration order.',
        'Sync throws are automatic lane changes; async rejections in Express 4 fall off the road entirely (no lane sees them) — which is why the asyncHandler on-ramp exists.',
      ],
      whatBreaksWithoutIt: 'Without the lane model, people expect regular middleware to run after next(err), or delete the "unused" next from an error handler and silently move it to the wrong lane.',
      interviewTip: 'Draw the two lanes when asked about error middleware — then drop the arity-detection fact and the Express 4 async hole as the two expert details.',
    },
    {
      id: 'mm-onion',
      title: 'The App as an Onion (layered architecture)',
      flow: ['HTTP edge: routes + middleware', 'Controllers: translate HTTP ↔ domain', 'Services: business rules, transactions', 'Repositories: data access', 'Database / external APIs'],
      explanation: [
        'Each layer only talks to the next one in. HTTP concepts (req, res, status codes) must not leak past controllers; SQL must not leak above repositories.',
        'The payoff is substitution: services tested without HTTP (no supertest needed for business logic), repositories swapped (Postgres → mock) without touching rules, a future GraphQL or queue consumer reusing the same services.',
        'Express only occupies the outer skin — which is exactly why "structure" questions are framework-agnostic and why answering them well signals seniority.',
      ],
      whatBreaksWithoutIt: 'Fat controllers where HTTP parsing, business rules, and SQL interleave — untestable without a running server, unreusable from jobs/queues, and risky to change.',
      interviewTip: 'One sentence per layer plus "req/res never crosses the controller boundary" answers the project-structure question completely.',
    },
    {
      id: 'mm-contract',
      title: 'REST API as a Published Contract',
      flow: ['Client reads the contract (docs/OpenAPI)', 'Request honors it (shape, auth, idempotency keys)', 'Server response honors it (codes, shapes, headers)', 'Changes → new version, old contract still honored', 'Retirement → deprecation headers, sunset, 410'],
      explanation: [
        'An API is a promise to strangers, not a function call you control both sides of. Status codes, response shapes, and error formats are contract clauses — 200-with-error-body is a breach, and breaking a shape without a version bump is too.',
        'Idempotency clauses tell clients what is safe to retry: GET/PUT/DELETE inherently, POST only with an Idempotency-Key mechanism you implement.',
        'Once published, you serve every version you ever shipped until measurement proves nobody is left — the contract outlives your refactors.',
      ],
      whatBreaksWithoutIt: 'Teams that treat endpoints as internal functions break mobile apps in the field, return 200 for failures, and make retries dangerous — the exact failure stories interviewers probe for.',
      interviewTip: 'Frame every REST-design answer as "what can the client rely on?" — it naturally produces correct codes, versioning, and idempotency reasoning.',
    },
  ],

