      id: 'r-error-handling',
      topic: 'Error middleware & response design',
      importance: 5,
      thirtySecond: 'One final (err, req, res, next) funnel: operational AppErrors → their status + safe message; unknown → 500 + generic body + full server log. Check headersSent. Consistent JSON error shape with a code and requestId.',
      twoMinute: 'Arity-4 detection means the unused next must stay declared. Map error types to HTTP: ZodError→400 with fields, JWT errors→401, AppError→statusCode, default→500. Log 5xx with stack and request context, 4xx as warn — alert on 5xx rate. notFound middleware before it routes 404s through the same funnel. Never expose stacks, SQL, or paths to clients; NODE_ENV=production also changes Express\'s own default handler.',
      deepDive: 'res.headersSent guards the streaming case — a response partially written when the error hits must delegate to the default handler (which destroys the connection) rather than attempting res.status. Error body design: machine-readable code, human message, requestId for support, optional fields[] — RFC 7807 problem+json is the standardized version worth citing.',
      followUps: ['Design the error JSON shape', 'Why might headersSent be true?'],
