      twoMinute: 'The boundary pattern: validate(schema) parses and replaces req parts with sanitized, typed output — controllers never see untrusted shapes. Unknown-key stripping blocks mass assignment (client setting role: \'admin\' on a profile update). Coercion handles params/query being strings. Validation errors flow through next(err) into the central handler, mapping to 400 with a fields array. Sanitization (trim, normalize email) is a separate concern often co-located.',
      deepDive: 'zod schemas as single source of truth: z.infer gives handler types, zod-to-openapi generates docs — one definition, three artifacts. Validate response shapes in tests too (contract testing). For file uploads and big bodies, limits are validation\'s sibling: reject by Content-Length/size limits before parsing costs you memory.',
      followUps: ['What is mass assignment and how does stripping prevent it?', 'Where do validation errors map in the error funnel?'],
