      twoMinute: 'JWT verification is stateless and cross-service (RS256 + JWKS when multiple verifiers); revocation needs short expiry + rotating refresh tokens or a denylist. Sessions invert the trade: revocation trivial, store required, CSRF protection needed because cookies auto-send. 401 = unauthenticated, 403 = authenticated but forbidden — interviewers check this. Mount-point guarding (router.use(authenticate, requireRole(\'admin\'))) protects subtrees declaratively.',
      deepDive: 'Order in the chain: after body parsing isn\'t required (token is in headers) but conventionally after CORS so preflights (which carry no credentials) aren\'t rejected. express-session MemoryStore is dev-only — leaks and per-process. Refresh rotation with reuse detection (a reused old refresh token = theft signal → revoke the family) is the expert add-on. AsyncLocalStorage can expose the user to deep layers without threading req.',
      followUps: ['Why does CORS order matter for auth?', '401 vs 403 with examples?'],
