      thirtySecond: 'Browser-enforced read protection. Non-simple requests (JSON, Authorization header, PUT/DELETE) preflight with OPTIONS; server must echo allowed origin/methods/headers. Credentials forbid wildcard origin. curl is unaffected.',
      twoMinute: 'Simple requests (GET/HEAD/POST with form-ish content types, no custom headers) skip preflight — and execute server-side even when the browser blocks reading the response. The cors middleware answers OPTIONS and stamps Access-Control-Allow-* on responses; configure an origin allowlist (function form for dynamic checks), credentials: true for cookies, maxAge to cache preflights (cutting request latency in half for chatty SPAs). Place before routes.',
      deepDive: 'CORS protects users from their credentials being exploited cross-site; it is NOT server access control — APIs still need auth. Vary: Origin must accompany dynamic allow-origins for cache correctness. Private Network Access and null origins (sandboxed iframes, file://) are modern edge cases. CSRF relates: SameSite cookies + CORS together shape the cross-site attack surface.',
      followUps: ['Why does the request still execute despite the CORS error?', 'What does maxAge optimize?'],
