      twoMinute: 'Idempotency by method: GET/PUT/DELETE repeat-safe, POST not — payment-style APIs accept an Idempotency-Key header, store key→response atomically (unique constraint or Redis SETNX), and replay the stored response on retries. Versioning: never break a shipped shape; run v1 and v2 routers over shared services with per-version presenters; measure adoption before retiring; Deprecation/Sunset headers and brownouts on the way out. Error responses are part of the contract — consistent shape, machine codes.',
      deepDive: 'The idempotency race: two concurrent retries both see "no key" — the write must be atomic with claim (insert with unique key first, then process). PATCH vs PUT semantics (partial vs full replacement; PATCH is not guaranteed idempotent). Pagination, filtering, and sorting conventions plus cache headers (ETag/If-None-Match for conditional GETs) round out the contract conversation.',
      followUps: ['Implement Idempotency-Key — where is the race?', 'PUT vs PATCH semantics?'],
