      thirtySecond: 'routes → controllers (HTTP only) → services (logic) → repositories (data); req/res never crosses the controller line. app.js builds, server.js listens. SIGTERM: fail readiness, server.close, drain, close pools, exit — under a hard timeout.',
      twoMinute: 'The layering pays in testability (services unit-tested without HTTP, supertest against the exported app without a port) and reuse (queue consumers calling the same services). Graceful shutdown order: readiness probe fails first so the LB drains; server.close() refuses new connections while in-flight requests finish; closeIdleConnections() drops keep-alive sockets that would otherwise hold close open; DB/Redis pools close; exit 0 — an unref\'d force-exit timer caps the whole sequence inside the orchestrator grace period.',
      deepDive: 'Keep-alive is the classic shutdown gotcha: idle sockets aren\'t "active requests" but block server.close until their timeout — pre-Node-18 you tracked sockets manually. Long-lived connections (SSE/WebSocket) need application-level goodbye messages. Liveness must keep passing during drain or the orchestrator kills you mid-flight. The app/server split also enables serverless adapters reusing the same app object.',
      whiteboard: ['SIGTERM', 'readiness → failing (LB drains)', 'server.close + closeIdleConnections', 'finish in-flight, close pools', 'exit 0 (force timer capped)'],
